903aa5ea31" :question/math-academy-id "q-141089" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :task-item/_content [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409299 :task-item/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272202} :task-item/elapsed-seconds 1599.34 :task-item/id #uuid "ee8d6d4e-9c87-4f8a-9830-8f44d0a92988" :task-item/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186577101} :task-item/responses [{:answer/id #uuid "3562e5cf-f895-5f46-86d8-1f4d377e852c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437968} {:answer/id #uuid "2c072334-a0f1-5100-a69e-a1a0ab21bc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437971} {